could put these 30 years of neuroscience and 40 years of this disorder together and maybe do something useful with that. BFRBs used to be classified as impulse control disorders, but they're now grouped under obsessive compulsive disorders in the DSM, the diagnostic. statistical manual used by psychologists and physicians. So are these behaviors a form of OCD? How do they differ from and overlap with other disorders? I mean, are they related to things like Tourette's syndrome, for example? Sure. Great question. You know, it's tough because I wish we could have a separate category for BFRBs. It would be so nice. And we really advocated for that in the last iteration of the DSM. And unfortunately, that didn't happen. It really didn't fit in impulse control disorders.

